| Concern | Decision |
|---|---|
| **Bulk safety** | The trigger iterates `Trigger.new` (up to 200+ records) and enqueues one lightweight Queueable per eligible case. No SOQL or DML in the trigger body, so it stays well within governor limits regardless of batch size. |
| **Transition detection** | `Trigger.oldMap` is used (O(1) lookup) to compare `IsClosed` old vs. new. Only the `false → true` edge fires the job, so re-opening a case or updating other fields on an already-closed case won't re-trigger it. |
| **Isolation** | Each case gets its own Queueable. If one job throws (e.g., a transient issue), only that case's survey task is missed; the other 199 still succeed. |
| **Data fetching in the job** | The job re-queries the Case by Id to get `CaseNumber` and `ContactId`. This is one lightweight SOQL per job and is fine for a Queueable (no sharing / bulk context needed). Passing more fields from the trigger is possible but would couple the job to the trigger's field list and complicate `System.enqueueJob` when the payload grows (the planned vendor integration). |
| **Null-contact guard in both layers** | The trigger skips cases with no contact *at trigger time*. The job re-checks after the query as a defensive guard for the race where a contact is removed between enqueue and execution. |
| **No re-entrancy risk** | Inserting a `Task` does not fire the `Case` trigger, so there is no loop. If a future change did update the Case from within the job, the transition guard (`!oldC.IsClosed && c.IsClosed`) would still prevent a second enqueue because `IsClosed` would already be `true` on both sides. |

